  <!-- One output root for every variant: out\<Platform>\<Configuration>\ for the
       binaries, with the compiler's intermediates tucked under obj\ inside it.
       Both are pinned unconditionally and anchored on $(ProjectDir), because the
       MSBuild defaults are neither stable nor tidy:
         - OutDir defaulted to $(SolutionDir)$(Platform)\$(Configuration)\, so the
           linked DLL moved depending on which solution drove the build.
         - IntDir defaulted to $(ShortProjectName)\$(Platform)\$(Configuration)\
           when SolutionDir was this project's own folder and to
           $(Platform)\$(Configuration)\ otherwise, which is how the old x64\Debug
           held intermediates but no DLL while a stray Msgcore\x64\Debug held
           the rest.
       Everything downstream is path-independent and needs no change: the deploy
       CustomBuildStep reads $(TargetDir), and the import library goes to
       $(WDMSCS_LIB) (MSCS\lib\<Platform>\<Configuration>) via Directory.Build.props. -->

  <!-- WHY EVERY CustomBuildStep BELOW DECLARES <Inputs>, and it is a
       correctness fix rather than tidiness. The step copies $(TargetPath) into
       the shared bin\<Cfg><Arch> tree, but until 2026-08-22 it declared only an
       <Outputs>, and the input added that day was spelled <AdditionalInputs>,
       which this step does not read - see THE NAME below - so the behaviour
       described here survived unchanged until 2026-09-12. With no input naming
       the thing being copied, MSBuild's incremental check compares the staged
       DLL against THE PROJECT FILE:

         Skipping target "CustomBuildStep" because all output files are
         up-to-date with respect to the input files.
           Input files:  Msgcore(2026).vcxproj
           Output files: ...\bin\Debug64\..\Debug64\Msgcore.dll

       A .vcxproj that has not been edited in a week is older than any staged
       copy, so the step never ran and the shared tree kept serving a stale DLL
       while the linker wrote a FRESH import library straight to $(WDMSCS_LIB).
       A current .lib against an old .dll, with no diagnostic - and every example
       tree links exactly that pair.

       -t:Rebuild was not a way out, and the reason it sometimes looked like one
       is worth knowing. Clean deletes outputs recorded in the previous build's
       write log; a step that has never run recorded nothing, so Clean leaves the
       stale file, so the step skips again. It is a latch: never run -> never
       logged -> never cleaned -> never run. Targetcore escaped only because its
       step had run once long ago, which is why Rebuild re-staged that one DLL
       and not this one - the projects were equally broken, not different.

       $(TargetPath) is the linked DLL in $(OutDir). Naming it as an input makes
       the comparison the honest one - freshly linked binary against staged copy
       - so the step re-runs on an ordinary incremental build and not only after
       a Clean.

       THE NAME. It has to be <Inputs>. Microsoft.CppCommon.targets declares

         <Target Name="CustomBuildStep"
                 Inputs="%(CustomBuildStep.Inputs);$(ProjectFileName)" ...>

       and nothing anywhere folds AdditionalInputs into that. AdditionalInputs
       is the per-file CustomBuild item's metadata; on the project-level build
       step it is accepted, written, and inert. The diagnosis above was right
       on 2026-08-22 and the fix still did nothing, because a wrong metadata
       name fails silently - there is no warning for metadata no one reads.
       To check rather than assume, ask MSBuild what it compared:

         msbuild <proj> -p:Configuration=Release -p:Platform=x64
                 -v:diag -t:CustomBuildStep | findstr /C:"Input files"

       A line naming only the .vcxproj means the input never arrived.

       KNOWN AND ACCEPTED: this makes the step run on EVERY build rather than
       only when the DLL changed. IW_CustomBuildStep.bat stages with `copy /y`,
       which preserves the source timestamp, so the staged copy is never
       STRICTLY newer than its input and MSBuild's check can never settle
       (measured: src and staged both 13:00:48.289 to the millisecond). Making
       it settle would mean touching the destination, and that batch is shared
       by 26 projects. The trade is a ~1 MB file copy against a C++ link, which
       is not worth a shared-file change - and always-copy is the stronger
       property for the failure being fixed here, since it also repairs a staged
       DLL that was replaced by hand. If the log line "Performing Custom Build
       Step" on a no-op build looks wrong, this is why. -->

